# Known differences for a MariaDB against MySQL run:
#   KNOWN_FILE=corlogic.known.mysql
# The line forms are the ones corlogic.known documents. Every entry below is a place where
# the two products promise different things, so a difference there is the dialect and not a
# defect. Nothing here is muted on a same-vendor run.
#
# A function only one of them has, and the same function used where the other cannot take
# it. GROUPING() is the one this turns up most.
ERROR|1305v0|*|*
ERROR|0v1305|*|*
ERROR|1221v0|*|*
ERROR|0v1221|*|*

# QUICK is a MariaDB keyword in DELETE. MySQL reads it as a table alias, so the same text
# is a different statement there and the WHERE clause compares another column.
ERROR|*|*|DELETE QUICK*

# An unused common table expression: MariaDB resolves its column names and MySQL does not,
# so a CTE that names a column it does not have is an error on one side only.
ERROR|1054v0|*CTE*|*
ERROR|0v1054|*CTE*|*

# MySQL refuses a subquery that reads the table the statement updates or deletes from.
ERROR|0v1093|*|*
ERROR|1093v0|*|*

# `utf8` is an alias for utf8mb3 on MySQL and for utf8mb4 on a recent MariaDB, so a
# statement that mixes an introducer literal with a binary literal aggregates to a
# different column type on each side. Measured against CS 13.1.0: MySQL 5.5, 5.6 and 5.7
# differ, 8.0.36 and 9.1.0 agree. As described in corlogic.known.INFO.
RESULT|coltype|*CSINTRO*|*

# Hexadecimal and binary string literals. MariaDB deliberately separates the two
# syntaxes: X'value' / x'value' is the SQL Standard binary string literal and stays a
# string in every context, while 0xvalue is the MySQL/MariaDB hex hybrid that acts as a
# number in a numeric context. MySQL treats both as numbers. Closed as expected behaviour
# three times - MDEV-6092, MDEV-10479 and MDEV-38434. As described in corlogic.known.INFO.
RESULT|content|*|*CONV(*
RESULT|content|*|*x'*

# GREATEST and LEAST given both a string and a number. MariaDB compares the arguments as
# numbers and MySQL compares them as strings, so each picks a different argument. The two
# entries name the two statements that were judged, because the same conversion carries a
# filed bug and a wider entry would mute that as well. As described in corlogic.known.INFO.
f05f2e49fd1f//RESULT|content|PLAIN|SELECT GREATEST(cX,cX,N) AS r0 FROM tX WHERE ABS(cX) <N
58b12e6fc964//RESULT|content|PLAIN|SELECT LEAST(cX,cX,N) AS r0 FROM t WHERE ABS(cX) <N

# WEIGHT_STRING() on a temporal value. MariaDB returns the bytes of the string form and
# MySQL returns the packed sort key. Over a string column both return collation weights, so
# only a temporal argument differs. As described in corlogic.known.INFO.
424985bb422a//RESULT|content|PLAIN|SELECT WEIGHT_STRING(cX) AS r0 FROM tX
